/**

@page peano_partition The partition

\tableofcontents

peano4::Spacetree owns the tree of one rank, cuts the tree along the
space-filling curve into one peano4::Partition per rank and runs every regrid.
An application reads the partition of its rank and stores its data in pools
indexed by the slots of that partition. There is no grid sweep: a phase runs
dispatches over the spans of the partition.

@image html documentation/peano4/partition.svg

The figure shows, from left to right, the spacetree cut along the curve into
the partitions of three ranks; the cells and faces of one partition with their
slots, the faces a neighbour rank holds too and one level jump; and the slot
moves of one regrid.

# Cells, faces and level jumps

- A cell is an unrefined cell of the tree the rank owns. Its slot survives
  every regrid that keeps the cell. peano4::Partition::getCellLevel(),
  peano4::Partition::getCellLattice(), peano4::Partition::getCellCentre() and
  peano4::Partition::getCellWidth() return its level, its integer lattice
  position on that level and the geometry the kernel derives from them.
  peano4::Partition::getCellFace() returns the slot of the face with number
  `n`; the faces below `DIMENSIONS` are the lower faces along the axes, the
  faces from `DIMENSIONS` on the upper ones.
- A face is numbered afresh by every regrid. peano4::Partition::getFaceKind()
  returns peano4::FaceKind::Interior, `Boundary`, `Hanging` (the fine face of
  a level jump), `Coarse` (the coarse face of a level jump) or
  `Intermediate` (a face of a chain of level jumps that no cell holds).
  peano4::Partition::getFaceCell() returns the cell on the lower or the upper
  side of the face; a side without a cell of this rank holds -1.
- A peano4::LevelJump names a coarse face, the side of its coarse cell and
  its fine faces in subface order.
- A face two ranks hold appears in the partition of both ranks, each with its
  own cell on one side. peano4::Partition::getNeighbours() lists the
  neighbour ranks and peano4::Partition::getExchangedFaces() the faces this
  rank and the neighbour hold, in the canonical order (level, normal, lattice)
  both ranks derive without agreeing on it.

peano4::Spacetree::getCellAt() returns the cell of a level and lattice
position, so an application finds the cell that holds a position.

# Spans

Every list of the partition is ordered so that a group an application loops
over is one contiguous span. A cell, face or level jump of
peano4::Adjacency::Local reads the data of this rank only, one of
peano4::Adjacency::Remote reads a face half a neighbour rank sends.

- peano4::Partition::getCells() returns every cell, `getCells(adjacency)`
  the local or the remote cells and `getCells(adjacency, level)` those of one
  level, for an application that steps the levels one at a time.
- peano4::Partition::getFaces() returns the local or the remote faces of one
  kind, or of a run of kinds in the order `Interior, Boundary, Hanging,
  Coarse, Intermediate`.
- peano4::Partition::getLevelJumps() returns the local or the remote level
  jumps, or those whose coarse face lies on one level.

The remote groups follow the local groups in every list, so an application
runs the local groups while the halo messages travel and the remote groups
after peano4::HaloExchange::wait().

# One step

The pseudocode names every call an application needs; `n` runs over the faces
of a cell and `solution` and `halo` are pools with one chunk per cell slot and
per face.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
const peano4::Partition& partition = peano4::Spacetree::getInstance().getPartition();
solution.reserve(partition.getNumberOfSlots());                // one chunk per cell slot
halo.reserve(partition.getNumberOfFaces());                    // one chunk per face
haloExchange.rebuild(partition);                               // the faces this rank and a neighbour hold
for (cell : partition.getCells())                writeFaceHalves(solution[cell], halo[partition.getCellFace(cell, n)]);
for (jump : partition.getLevelJumps(Local))      interpolate(halo[jump.coarseFace], halo[jump.fineFaces]);
haloExchange.post();
for (cell : partition.getCells(Local))           update(solution[cell], halo[partition.getCellFace(cell, n)]);
haloExchange.wait();
for (jump : partition.getLevelJumps(Remote))     interpolate(halo[jump.coarseFace], halo[jump.fineFaces]);
for (cell : partition.getCells(Remote))          update(solution[cell], halo[partition.getCellFace(cell, n)]);
peano4::Regrid regrid;
if (peano4::Spacetree::getInstance().regrid(requests, regrid)) { migration.migrate(solution, regrid, transfer); }
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

# Memory

The host reads the partition of peano4::Spacetree::getPartition(). A kernel
copies peano4::Partition::onDevice() by value and reads one cell or face
through the getters that take a slot or a face; in a CPU build both are the
same object. Every array exists once per memory location that reads it.

Every data type of an application is one tarch::accelerator::ChunkedMemoryPool
per rank, allocated in device memory when a GPU backend is active and in heap
memory otherwise. `pool.getChunk(slot)` is the data of the entity at that
slot.

# The regrid

peano4::Spacetree::regrid() applies a list of peano4::RefinementRequest
records, rebuilds the partition and fills a peano4::Regrid: the prolongations
(the parent's old slot, the child's new slot and its position), the
restrictions (the parent's new slot and the children's old slots), the sends
and receives per neighbour rank in canonical order, the slots the regrid frees
and the slots it creates. A new slot never reuses a slot freed in the same
regrid, so every dispatch of the transfer reads and writes disjoint slots of
the same pool. peano4::MigrationExchange::migrate() moves one pool through the
record; peano4::Spacetree::finishRegrid() closes the regrid once every pool
has moved.

*/
